<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaykh Haydar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1459–1488</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shaykh Haydar was head of the Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> from 1460 to 1488 and father of Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>. Married to a daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>, he turned the order's followers into a fighting force and is credited with the red headgear that gave them the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>. He was killed in 1488 fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukh-yasar">Farrukh Yasar</a> and his Aq Qoyunlu allies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar.jpg" alt="Shaykh Haydar" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 16th century artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheikh_Haydar_Husayni,_and_Sultan_%CA%BFAli_Safavi_(Brother_of_Isma%CA%BFil_I),_detail._Cem%CA%BF-i_T%C4%81r%C4%ABh,_Museum_of_Ethnography,_Ankara,_No._8457,_fol._17a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
